      <h3 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
        2. The Anatomy of GHS Codes: H-Codes vs. P-Codes
      </h3>
      <p>
        Hazard statements are alphanumeric codes consisting of the letter <strong>H</strong> followed by three digits:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;">
        <li><strong>H200–H299:</strong> Physical Hazards (Explosives, Flammables, Self-Reactives, Pyrophorics, Oxidizers, Corrosive to Metals).</li>
        <li><strong>H300–H399:</strong> Health Hazards (Acute Oral/Dermal Toxicity, Skin Corrosion, Eye Damage, Mutagenicity, Carcinogenicity, Reproductive Toxicity, STOT).</li>
        <li><strong>H400–H499:</strong> Environmental Hazards (Acute and Chronic Aquatic Toxicity, Ozone Depletion).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Precautionary statements follow the letter <strong>P</strong> followed by three digits:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;">
        <li><strong>P100 Series:</strong> General precautions (keep out of reach of children, read label).</li>
        <li><strong>P200 Series:</strong> Prevention measures (wear PPE, avoid open flames, use in well-ventilated areas).</li>
        <li><strong>P300 Series:</strong> Response protocols (first aid, eye rinsing, poison center contact).</li>
        <li><strong>P400 Series:</strong> Safe storage protocols (keep locked, store in cool, ventilated area).</li>
        <li><strong>P500 Series:</strong> Proper hazardous waste disposal procedures.</li>
      </ul>

